    dict(id="P04-09", file="Chapter4_TRF_Model_v1.md",
         old="*The interpretive condition on which Corollary 1.2 depends.* The corollary holds if, and only if, the deletion obligation of Article 68(12) reaches the identifiable access logs that Article 73(1)(e) requires to be kept. That condition is contestable and must be stated as such rather than assumed, because the corollary is the sharpest claim in this thesis and a reader is entitled to see its weakest joint.\n\nThe textual case for the broad reading is that the provision governs *the data within the secure processing environment*, a formulation defined by location rather than by provenance. It does not say \"the data made available under the data permit,\" though the Regulation uses that narrower formulation freely elsewhere, and the access logs are indisputably data within the environment. On that reading Article 68(12) reaches them, and Corollary 1.2 follows.",
         new="*The interpretive condition on which Corollary 1.2 depends.* The corollary holds if, and only if, the deletion obligation of Article 68(12) reaches the identifiable access logs that Article 73(1)(e) requires to be kept. That condition is contestable and is stated as such.\n\nArticle 68(12) provides that \"the electronic health data within the secure processing environment shall be deleted within six months of the expiry of the data permit\". Its scope is set by location rather than by provenance: it does not say \"the data made available under the data permit\". Its object, however, is electronic health data, which Article 2(2)(a) and (c) define as \"data concerning health and genetic data, processed in an electronic form\", personal or non-personal. An access log is therefore not reached merely because it sits within the environment. An entry recording that an authorised researcher opened a dataset at a given time concerns the researcher, not anyone's health, and Article 73(1) ties the identifiability of logs to the persons who access: access is restricted to \"authorised natural persons listed in the data permit\" (point (a)), and the handling of data to \"authorised identifiable individuals\" (point (c)). An entry kept at patient-record granularity is different. Recording that a named patient's record in a disease cohort was accessed reveals that the patient belongs to the cohort, which is data concerning health. Such entries are electronic health data within the environment, and on the broad reading, which takes the provision's scope from its location, Article 68(12) reaches them, and Corollary 1.2 follows for them.",
         why="M5c verbatim; M2a: the textual case rebuilt on Art 68(12)'s actual object and Art 2(2), 73(1)(a),(c) (D-16)"),
